{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PTLNbYBHqhLeuUErosmATM","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tsource_url: \"https://github.com/cncf/curriculum/blob/master/KCNA_Curriculum.pdf\"\n3\tfetched_at: \"2026-08-23T22:30:00-0400\"\n4\tauthority: \"Cloud Native Computing Foundation (cncf/curriculum)\"\n5\tobjectives_covered: [\"D1\", \"D2\", \"D3\", \"D4\"]\n6\tconcepts_covered: [\"kcna-competencies\"]\n7\t---\n8\t# Kubernetes and Cloud Native Associate (KCNA) Exam Curriculum — CNCF publication (text extracted from KCNA_Curriculum.pdf, 3 pages)\n9\t\n10\t\"This document provides the curriculum outline of the Knowledge, Skills and Abilities that a Kubernetes and Cloud Native Associate (KCNA) can be expected to demonstrate.\"\n11\t\n12\t## KCNA Curriculum\n13\t- 44% – Kubernetes Fundamentals: Kubernetes Core Concepts; Administration; Scheduling; Containerization\n14\t- 28% – Container Orchestration: Networking; Security; Troubleshooting; Storage\n15\t- 16% – Cloud Native Application Delivery: Application Delivery; Debugging\n16\t- 12% – Cloud Native Architecture: Observability; Cloud Native Ecosystem and Principles; Cloud Native Community and Collaboration\n17\t\n18\t(The PDF contains the typo \"Could Native Community and Collaboration\" for the last competency.)\n19\t\n20\t\"Cloud native computing uses an open source software stack to deploy applications as microservices, packaging each part into its own container, and dynamically orchestrating those containers to optimize resource utilization. The Cloud Native Computing Foundation (CNCF) hosts critical components of those software stacks including Kubernetes, Fluentd, Linkerd, Prometheus, OpenTracing and gRPC; brings together the industry's top developers, end users, and vendors; and serves as a neutral home for collaboration. CNCF is part of The Linux Foundation, a nonprofit organization.\"\n21\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2c1f0b2c-853c-4751-83c7-0a0011fe7adc","uuid":"b6b6c2d9-afcd-473f-81af-8d5f6176c596","timestamp":"2026-09-01T00:15:08.274Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\sources\\cncf-kcna-curriculum-pdf-2026-08-23.md","content":"---\nsource_url: \"https://github.com/cncf/curriculum/blob/master/KCNA_Curriculum.pdf\"\nfetched_at: \"2026-08-23T22:30:00-0400\"\nauthority: \"Cloud Native Computing Foundation (cncf/curriculum)\"\nobjectives_covered: [\"D1\", \"D2\", \"D3\", \"D4\"]\nconcepts_covered: [\"kcna-competencies\"]\n---\n# Kubernetes and Cloud Native Associate (KCNA) Exam Curriculum — CNCF publication (text extracted from KCNA_Curriculum.pdf, 3 pages)\n\n\"This document provides the curriculum outline of the Knowledge, Skills and Abilities that a Kubernetes and Cloud Native Associate (KCNA) can be expected to demonstrate.\"\n\n## KCNA Curriculum\n- 44% – Kubernetes Fundamentals: Kubernetes Core Concepts; Administration; Scheduling; Containerization\n- 28% – Container Orchestration: Networking; Security; Troubleshooting; Storage\n- 16% – Cloud Native Application Delivery: Application Delivery; Debugging\n- 12% – Cloud Native Architecture: Observability; Cloud Native Ecosystem and Principles; Cloud Native Community and Collaboration\n\n(The PDF contains the typo \"Could Native Community and Collaboration\" for the last competency.)\n\n\"Cloud native computing uses an open source software stack to deploy applications as microservices, packaging each part into its own container, and dynamically orchestrating those containers to optimize resource utilization. The Cloud Native Computing Foundation (CNCF) hosts critical components of those software stacks including Kubernetes, Fluentd, Linkerd, Prometheus, OpenTracing and gRPC; brings together the industry's top developers, end users, and vendors; and serves as a neutral home for collaboration. CNCF is part of The Linux Foundation, a nonprofit organization.\"\n","numLines":21,"startLine":1,"totalLines":21}}}
